                  m{\'e}di{\'e}vales},
  number       = {2},
  pages	       = {427--447},
  publisher    = {Peeters Publishers},
  title	       = {Connections {{Between Metaphysics}} and {{Logic}} in
                  {{Ockham}}'s {{Thought}}},
  url	       = {https://www.jstor.org/stable/26485488},
  volume       = {79},
  year	       = {2012},
  abstract     = {Ockham's interest in logic is indubitable. His
                  interest in metaphysics, as opposed to individual
                  metaphysical issues, is less obvious. This paper
                  seeks to compare and contrast Ockham's view of
                  metaphysics and logic as scientiae on a number of
                  points with the aim of calling attention to what is
                  distinctive about the reach and content of
                  metaphysical research. Whereas the logician studies
                  terms, propositions, and syllogisms, the
                  metaphysician sets out and defends a general
                  ontology. Of particular note are two areas where
                  metaphysics and logic overlap, namely the categories
                  and the concept.}
}

